<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.1.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- pi-cache-reap (#1987): hourly bound on the MACHINE-GLOBAL package caches.

         WHY. `pi-cell-3` reached 258MB free on a 32GB disk and refused every
         dispatch; `~/.cache/uv` alone was 7.1GB and clearing it freed the box
         instantly (258MB -> 7.7GB free). The cache is not per-lane — every
         `uv sync`/`uv run` on the box adds to it — and nothing bounded it:
         pi-reap-worktrees.sh (#1095) reaps WORKTREES, and the cell supervisor's
         `prune` sweeps per-worktree `.venv`/`node_modules`, so neither can reach
         `$HOME/.cache/uv`. Same class of sink: `~/.npm/_cacache` and
         `~/.cache/pip`.

         SHIPS IN DRY-RUN, LIKE EVERY SIBLING REAPER. This job is INSTALLED by
         default and hourly, and it only REPORTS until it is armed:
         `com.tortoise.worktree-reaper` and `com.eldato.pi-task-session-prune`
         both ship in a dry run, and the reason is a recorded OWNER ruling
         (agent-infra#1678, 2026-10-11): "the reaper may run unattended; arming it
         may not", and "Arming a deletion tool must not be reachable by a lane at
         all". The reaper's own gates are what make an armed pass SAFE — they are a
         correctness argument, not an authorisation; the authorisation is the
         owner's, because arming is a deliberate, per-machine consequence decision.

         ARMING (owner action, one argument, reversible):
           1. replace the dry-run argument below with the apply argument.
              ⛔ Do NOT reach for CACHE_REAP_APPLY=1 here instead: the script lets an
              explicit flag win over that env default (so a requested dry run really
              is one), which means the variable CANNOT arm a job whose
              ProgramArguments still say dry-run — it would report itself installed
              and loaded while doing nothing, the inert-enforcer shape #1678 removed.
           2. re-run scripts/install-launchd.sh
         The armed copy is then a dated, auditable state — re-rendered from this
         template on every sync, so there is no second copy to drift.
         ⛔ Do NOT "tidy" an armed install back to dry-run without the owner: the
         measured incident this job exists for (7.1GB -> 258MB free -> every
         dispatch refused) recurs as soon as nothing bounds the cache.

         What an armed pass may do (scripts/pi-cache-reap.sh, one owner of the
         policy): `uv cache prune`/`npm cache verify` first (the safe half, no
         deletion of live entries), then — only while NO live process of that tool
         holds the cache — remove the largest entries, preserving anything still
         hardlinked (unlinking it frees nothing) and anything on another filesystem
         (never delete through a mount). A pass that acted must PROVE the shrink
         (cache size down AND filesystem free space up by >=50% of the claim) or it
         exits 3; two concurrent passes are excluded by a lock.

         Canonical script: agent-infra/scripts/pi-cache-reap.sh
         (COPIED to ~/.pi/agent/scripts/ by pi-bootstrap/setup.sh's fleet-scripts
         farm; #427: launchd cannot read ~/Documents repos under macOS TCC —
         and this script reads only $HOME, so it runs under plain bash).
         Rendered by scripts/install-launchd.sh (#304) — placeholders only.

         TWO CLOCKS, ONE CACHE. This plist is the Mac's timer; the orchestrator
         harness (scripts/orchestrator-harness/orch-loop.sh) also runs the same
         script hourly on the same Mac AND on a Linux cell, which has no launchd. A
         pass is idempotent and cheap when a cache is under its bound, and both
         triggers ship dry-run; two overlapping ARMED passes cannot corrupt each
         other's before/after free-space measurement because the script takes a lock
         (`~/.pi/agent/state/pi-cache-reap.lock`).

         BUDGET. Hourly: growth is proportional to dispatch volume (the measured
         box went 0 -> 7.1GB), and the pass is cheap when under the bound (a `du`
         per cache and nothing else). The bound is per cache —
         CACHE_REAP_MAX_MB=2048, i.e. ~2GB, the value #1987 starts at.

         No StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath: the pass writes its own capped log
         at ~/.pi/agent/state/pi-cache-reap.log (an unbounded launchd-captured
         log would be the same disk sink this job exists to bound). -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.eldato.pi-cache-reap</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/bin/env</string>
        <string>bash</string>
        <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/pi-cache-reap.sh</string>
        <string>--dry-run</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <!-- In the plist, not inherited: a launchd job gets neither the shell's
             env nor its rc files, so an unset bound here would silently fall back
             to the script default — the same value, but pinned where a machine
             can be tuned from source. The bring-up report and the supervisor read
             the bound from the script (its print-config mode), so this is one of the
             two places it may legitimately be tuned (the other: the default).
             ⚠️ Tuning the bound HERE binds THIS job only: the bring-up report and the
             supervisor resolve the bound from the environment THEY run in, and
             its print-config mode prints the `bound_source` it used so the two cannot be
             read as the same policy when they differ. -->
        <key>CACHE_REAP_MAX_MB</key>
        <string>2048</string>
    </dict>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>3600</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
